-- title:
--   §2 and figure, channel region and real multiplicativity region
-- statement:
--   Let $d\ge1$, $H=\mathbb C^{\mathbb Z_d}$, and $Z=\mathbb Z_d\times\mathbb Z_d$. In the specified orthonormal basis let $W_{(x,y)}=U^xV^y$, where $Ue_k=e_{k+1}$ and $Ve_k=e^{2\pi i k/d}e_k$. Write $f_X(z)=\operatorname{Tr}(XW_z^*)/d$. All spaces are finite dimensional. The norm $\|X\|_2=\sqrt{\operatorname{Tr}(X^*X)}$ is the Hilbert–Schmidt norm, and $\nu_2(\Phi)=\sup_{\rho\ge0,\operatorname{Tr}\rho=1}\|\Phi(\rho)\|_2$ is the maximal output norm. A map in $W_1(H)$ is complex linear, Weyl covariant and satisfies $\Phi(I)=I$; it need not be positive or completely positive. Its computed symbol is $\phi(z)=f_{\Phi(W_z)}(z)$. Put $m(\phi)=\max_{z\ne0}|\phi(z)|^2$ and $E_{\max}(\phi)=\{z\ne0:|\phi(z)|^2=m(\phi)\}$. At $d=1$, explicitly set $m=0$ and $E_{\max}=\varnothing$. Write $S(\phi)=d^{-1/2}\sqrt{1+(d-1)m(\phi)}$.
--
--   The alternating pairing is $[u,v]=u_xv_y-u_yv_x\in\mathbb Z_d$. A subset is degenerate when this pairing vanishes for every pair of its elements. A maximal degenerate subgroup is inclusion maximal among degenerate subsets. For a unit vector $\psi$, define $G_\psi=\{z:\exists c\in\mathbb C,\ W_z\psi=c\psi\}$. Let $G(z)$ be the additive subgroup generated by $z$.
--
--   For an additive subgroup $G\le Z$, let $J(x,y)=(y,-x)$, $\chi(\gamma,z)=\exp(2\pi i(\gamma_xz_x+\gamma_yz_y)/d)$, and $G^\perp=\{\gamma:\chi(\gamma,z)=1\text{ for every }z\in G\}$. The symbol $\phi_{G,a,b}$ is $1$ at zero, $a+b$ on $G\setminus\{0\}$, and $b$ off $G$. Define $\Psi_G(X)=d^{-1}\sum_{z\in G^\perp}W_{Jz}XW_{Jz}^*$ and $\Phi_{G,a,b}(X)=a\Psi_G(X)+bX+(1-a-b)\operatorname{Tr}(X)I/d$.
--
--   Assume $d\ge2$, $|G|=d$, and $a,b\in\mathbb R$. If $(a,b)$ lies in the closed convex hull of
--
--   $$ (0,1),\quad \left(-\frac1{d-1},0\right),\quad\left(\frac d{d-1},-\frac1{d-1}\right),$$
--
--   then every Fourier coefficient $p_\gamma$ is real and nonnegative, their sum is one, and $\Phi_{G,a,b}$ is a channel. For real parameters, $|a+b|\ge|b|$ is equivalent to $a(a+2b)\ge0$. In the triangle, if $G$ is maximal degenerate and this inequality holds, multiplicativity follows with every finite-dimensional CP partner.
--
--   This states the closed channel region and the real sufficient multiplicativity region; $d=1$ is excluded because these vertices contain $1/(d-1)$.
-- source:
--   On Weyl-covariant channels, arXiv:quant-ph/0510148v3, PDF p. 10, §2 and figure, channel region and real multiplicativity region

import Definitions.Def_QAlgorithms_Papers_Independent_Bridge50_P05

